<Layout
    title="How to Save Tax in Pakistan (2026-27): 7 Legal Ways"
    description="Discover 7 legal ways to save income tax in Pakistan for Tax Year 2027. Includes VPS, life insurance, donations, and other FBR-approved tax credits."
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <header class="mb-8">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">
                Published: October 14, 2026 · Last Updated: October 24, 2026 ·
                By <a href="/about/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                    >Khuram Shahzad</a
                >
            </p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
                How to Save Tax in Pakistan (2026-27): 7 Legal Ways
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
                Every rupee you legally save on tax is a rupee back in your
                pocket. Here are seven FBR-approved ways salaried Pakistanis can
                reduce their tax bill this year.
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                Many salaried Pakistanis file their income tax return without
                realizing they could have saved significantly. A common
                scenario: you hand your documents to a tax consultant, and he
                asks one simple question — "Did you invest in anything this
                year?"
            </p>
            <p>
                Most people say no. And that's when they discover how much they
                could have saved — just by using tax credits they didn't know
                existed. Understanding these credits changes how you plan your
                finances every year.
            </p>
            <p>
                Pakistan's tax system actually rewards you for saving and
                investing. The government has built in several legal ways to
                reduce your tax liability. Most salaried people never use them
                because they simply don't know about them. This guide changes
                that.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Quick Answer: The 7 Tax Saving Options
            </h2>
            <p>
                Before we get into the details, here's the complete list of
                legal tax-saving methods available to salaried individuals in
                Pakistan for Tax Year 2027:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Voluntary Pension Scheme (VPS) contributions</li>
                <li>Life insurance premiums</li>
                <li>Charitable donations (Section 61)</li>
                <li>Zakat payments</li>
                <li>Investment in shares</li>
                <li>House loan markup</li>
                <li>Medical allowance structuring</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Not all of these will apply to everyone. But even using two or
                three of them can save you tens of thousands of rupees every
                year.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                1. Voluntary Pension Scheme (VPS) — The Most Underused Benefit
            </h2>
            <p>
                This is the one I wish I had known about earlier. The Voluntary
                Pension Scheme (VPS) is a retirement savings account that
                belongs entirely to you. It's portable across employers,
                flexible in how much you contribute, and you choose how the
                money is invested.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>How the tax credit works:</strong> Under Section 63 of the
                Income Tax Ordinance, your eligible contribution is the lower of your
                actual VPS contribution or 20% of your taxable income. You then multiply
                that eligible amount by your average tax rate.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Example:</strong> If your taxable income is PKR 1,000,000
                and your average tax rate is 15%, and you contribute PKR 180,000 to
                a VPS, your tax credit is PKR 180,000 × 15% = <strong
                    >PKR 27,000</strong
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                The larger your income, the bigger the absolute rupee savings.
                And the best part: while your tax is reduced, your money is
                still invested and growing for your retirement.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>How to claim it:</strong> If you're salaried, share your VPS
                contribution statement with your HR or Finance department. If you're
                self-employed, declare it when filing your return.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                2. Life Insurance Premiums — Tax Credit Under Section 62
            </h2>
            <p>
                If you pay premiums on a life insurance policy registered with
                the Securities and Exchange Commission of Pakistan (SECP), you
                qualify for a tax credit under Section 62.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>How it works:</strong> The eligible amount is the lower of:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Total premium paid during the year</li>
                <li>15% of your taxable income</li>
                <li>PKR 500,000</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Example:</strong> If your annual taxable income is PKR 1,080,000
                and your tax liability is PKR 108,000, and you pay PKR 150,000 in
                life insurance premiums, your tax credit is (108,000 / 1,080,000)
                × 150,000 = <strong>PKR 15,000</strong>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Important:</strong> You can claim tax credit on either your
                life insurance premium <em>or</em> your investment in shares — not
                both.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                3. Charitable Donations — Tax Credit Under Section 61
            </h2>
            <p>
                Pakistan's tax law rewards generosity. When you donate to an
                approved non-profit organization, educational institution,
                hospital, or relief fund, you can claim a tax credit .
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>How it works:</strong> The tax credit is allowed on the lower
                of:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>The donation amount</li>
                <li>
                    30% of your taxable income (for donations to approved NPOs)
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Important change:</strong> Since the Finance Act 2021, donations
                that previously qualified for direct deduction now qualify for tax
                credit instead. The list of eligible organizations is now in the Thirteenth
                Schedule of the Income Tax Ordinance.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Example:</strong> If you donate PKR 100,000 to an approved
                charity and your taxable income is PKR 2,000,000, you can claim a
                tax credit of PKR 100,000 × your average tax rate.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                4. Zakat Payments — Straight Deduction
            </h2>
            <p>
                Zakat paid under the Zakat and Usher Ordinance qualifies for a
                special straight deduction from your taxable income . This is
                different from a tax credit — it reduces your taxable income
                directly.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>How it works:</strong> If you paid PKR 150,000 as Zakat during
                the year, your taxable income is reduced by that amount before tax
                is calculated.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Example:</strong> Taxable salary of PKR 3,000,000 minus Zakat
                of PKR 150,000 = Revised taxable salary of PKR 2,850,000. Your tax
                is calculated on the lower amount.
            </p>
            <p>
                This is one of the simplest and most effective tax-saving
                methods available. If you pay Zakat regularly, make sure you're
                claiming it in your tax return.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                5. Investment in Shares — Tax Credit Under Section 62
            </h2>
            <p>
                If you invest in new shares offered to the public by a public
                company listed on a stock exchange in Pakistan, you can claim a
                tax credit. You must be the original allottee of the shares .
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Eligible amount:</strong> The lower of:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Total cost of acquiring the shares</li>
                <li>15% of your taxable income</li>
                <li>PKR 500,000</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Important condition:</strong> If you dispose of the shares
                within 36 months of acquisition, the tax credit is reversed, and your
                tax payable for the disposal year is increased by the credit amount
                .
            </p>
            <p>
                This option is best for long-term investors. If you're looking
                for quick gains, the tax penalty makes it less attractive.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                6. House Loan Markup — Tax Credit for Homeowners
            </h2>
            <p>
                If you've taken a loan from a scheduled bank, regulated
                financial institution, government, or listed company to
                construct or acquire a personal house, you can claim a tax
                credit on the markup or interest paid.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Property size limits:</strong>
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>House: Up to 2,500 square feet</li>
                <li>Flat: Up to 2,000 square feet</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <strong>Important:</strong> Once you claim this tax credit for one
                house or flat, you cannot claim it for another house or flat for the
                next 15 tax years. Plan accordingly.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                7. Medical Allowance — Structure Your Salary Right
            </h2>
            <p>
                This isn't a tax credit, but it's a valuable exemption. If your
                employer provides a medical allowance, up to 10% of your basic
                salary is exempt from tax — provided your employer doesn't
                separately reimburse medical expenses.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Example:</strong> If your basic salary is PKR 100,000 per
                month, up to PKR 10,000 per month as medical allowance can be tax-free.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Action point:</strong> Talk to your HR department. Many employers
                structure salaries in a way that maximizes tax exemptions — but they
                won't do it unless you ask.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Comparison: Which Tax Saving Option Is Best for You?
            </h2>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
